## Ownership and execution controls

I exclusively integrates shared files and schedules emulator/Flutter runtime access. All original 33 ownership assignments are retained. Two additive I-owned compatibility locks cover the location regression and existing notification-delivery source, The reviewed count-maintenance candidate adds explicitly owned producer paths before any source dispatch; the current registry has 75 unique entries (including the real RSS emulator fixture). Count-maintenance source scope is independently approved, but implementation gates and batch prerequisites remain.

The updated task, milestone and checkpoint graph has 87 acyclic nodes, including the partial Rules milestone and no unknown dependency reference. No original task, batch or wave was removed. Manifest implementationState distinguishes local handoffs from final pending acceptance; it is not a finding-closure claim.

The approved failure policy repairs routine in-scope failures and reruns affected gates inside the active batch. Downstream work remains blocked until its required local handoff is GREEN. Unresolved security issues, unowned scope expansion and production operations stop progression.

## Approved product policies

- Alerts honor each accepted schedule's UTC offset, with conservative sent/unknown receipt preservation during a later authorized cutover.
- Admin search offers clearly labeled current-page search plus an explicit resumable full-dataset scan, at most 200 documents per continuation.
- Preserve the existing architecture and dependencies. No new abstraction or dependency is authorized merely to simplify orchestration.

## Remaining acceptance obligations

All R01–R13 findings remain OPEN. Full Flutter tests, physical Android/iPhone acceptance, exact-candidate integration, independent Work Order reviews and demo migration rehearsals remain mandatory. All 17 original admin emulator identities passed in the corrected B03 run, together with three privacy cases; B15 must still prove the actual worker/harness integration and final G-INTEGRATION must rerun affected gates. Ordinary unit-run skips are not passing evidence.

Production mutation paths are not exercised. Privacy tooling must fail closed without verified encrypted backup, integrity, retention and scope safeguards; operational approval and any production adapter remain separate checkpoints. Count reconciliation cannot be called production-ready while enforced freeze proof is unavailable.

